Strong Final Interview Answer

Snowflake Virtual Warehouses use a T-shirt sizing model ranging from XSMALL to 6XLARGE, where each size approximately doubles compute resources such as CPU and memory. Larger warehouses generally improve performance for complex queries and large datasets, while simple queries may see little benefit. Warehouses are billed based on size and runtime rather than query count, with per-second billing after an initial 60-second minimum charge. Snowflake recommends workload-based experimentation to determine optimal warehouse sizing, and for most ingestion workloads, smaller warehouses are usually sufficient because loading performance depends more on file concurrency than raw compute power.
